ewed journals\n\nJournals such as the 'ANNALS OF SURGERY' and 'JPEN JOURNAL OF PARENTERAL AND ENTERAL NUTRITION' serve as critical platforms for disseminating research findings related to surgical nutrition and immunonutrition. These publications play a significant role in advancing knowledge and best practices in the field, influencing both clinical guidelines and the education of healthcare professionals. The impact of these journals is evident in the ongoing research and discussions surrounding nutritional support in surgical contexts. [Data: Entities (4729, 12927); Relationships (14013, 16048)]", "# Enhanced Recovery After Surgery Community\n\nThe Enhanced Recovery After Surgery (ERAS) community is centered around a set of protocols designed to improve recovery outcomes for patients undergoing elective surgeries. Key entities include ERAS itself, various surgical procedures, and countries contributing to the initiative, such as Norway, Scotland, and the Netherlands. The relationships among these entities highlight the importance of nutritional support, multidisciplinary integration, and the focus on reducing morbidity and hospitalization costs.\n\n## ERAS as a comprehensive recovery protocol\n\nEnhanced Recovery After Surgery (ERAS) is a comprehensive set of recommendations aimed at improving recovery for patients undergoing elective surgery. The program focuses on multimodal rehabilitation protocols that enhance patient outcomes by addressing various aspects of perioperative care. This includes nutritional support, pain management, and early mobilization, which are critical for optimizing recovery processes. The implementation of ERAS protocols has been shown to reduce length of hospital stays and associated costs, making it a vital component of modern surgical practices [Data: Entities (12584, 12583); Relationships (19196, 11388)].\n\n## The role of nutrition in ERAS\n\nNutrition plays a pivotal role in the ERAS protocols, emphasizing the importance of nutritional support for improving surgical outcomes. The ERAS recommendations include the use of enteral nutrition to enhance recovery post-surgery, particularly for patients at risk of malnutrition. This focus on nutrition is crucial, especially for older adults who may experience a decline in muscle mass during hospitalization. By addressing nutritional needs early in the hospitalization process, ERAS aims to optimize recovery and reduce complications [Data: Entities (12594, 12592); Relationships (2789, 3755)].\n\n## International collaboration in ERAS\n\nNorway, Scotland, and the Netherlands are among the northern European countries that have significantly contributed to the establishment and development of the ERAS initiative. These countries have collaborated to create standardized protocols that enhance surgical recovery, demonstrating a commitment to improving health outcomes on an international scale. Norway's involvement in data collection for the WHO's international growth reference patterns further underscores its role in advancing global health standards [Data: Entities (12586, 12584); Relationships (19194, 19193, 19195)].\n\n## Impact of ERAS on morbidity\n\nThe ERAS program aims to reduce morbidity associated with surgical procedures by implementing evidence-based practices that enhance recovery. By focusing on factors such as pain management, nutrition, and early mobilization, ERAS protocols seek to minimize complications and improve overall patient health. This focus on reducing morbidity is particularly important for high-risk surgical patients, as it can lead to better long-term outcomes and quality of life [Data: Entities (12593, 12592); Relationships (19201)].\n\n## Multidisciplinary integration in ERAS\n\nSuccessful implementation of ERAS protocols requires multidisciplinary integration among healthcare professionals. This collaboration is essential for addressing the various aspects of patient care, from preoperative assessments to postoperative recovery strategies. By fostering teamwork among surgeons, anesthesiologists, nurses, and nutritionists, ERAS aims to create a comprehensive care plan that optimizes recovery and enhances patient outcomes [Data: Entities (12598, 12590); Relationships (19205)].", "# Nutrition and Clinical Care Community\n\nThe community is centered around the field of nutrition, with key entities including prominent researchers, journals, and organizations that contribute to the understanding and application of nutrition in clinical settings. The relationships among these entities highlight the critical role of nutrition in managing various health conditions, including liver disease, cancer, and obesity.\n\n## Lochs H's influence on enteral nutrition guidelines\n\nLochs H is a prominent researcher known for co-authoring the ESPEN Guidelines on enteral nutrition, which provide expert recommendations crucial for clinical nutrition practices. His work significantly impacts how nutritional support is administered, particularly for patients with liver cirrhosis, emphasizing the importance of tailored nutritional strategies in clinical settings. The guidelines he contributed to are widely recognized and utilized in various healthcare contexts, influencing practices related to parenteral nutrition as well [Data: Entities (2261); Relationships (4652)].\n\n## Nutrition's role in disease management\n\nNutrition is a fundamental aspect of managing various health conditions, including diabetes, cancer, and cardiovascular diseases. The relationships among entities in this community illustrate how nutrition is integral to treatment protocols and recovery processes. For instance, nutrition plays a critical role in the management of cancer patients, particularly in addressing cachexia, and is essential for recovery in surgical patients. This highlights the necessity of incorporating nutritional assessments and interventions in clinical care [Data: Entities (1431); Relationships (5750, 3789, 6349)].\n\n##